# Each word tells the reader a step is easy or a behavior is small
# (`simply`, `merely`, `easily`, `straightforward`, `just run`), which
# the reader judges for themselves. None needs a guard for a product
# name or a mention of the word, since both are rare in technical
# prose, and the skill wants the words gone from a sentence about
# behavior.
#
# `simply` skips a following ` put`, since the restatement rule
# reports `Simply put`. The guard sits glued to the word so it reads
# the token right after it. `Put simply` and `To put it simply` count
# here instead, because no pattern can look back from `simply` to the
# `put` before it.
#
# After a negation the word carries a contrast (`it is not simply an
# offset`), and deleting it flips the claim, so the not-only rule's
# shape applies instead. Weir has no lookbehind, so the first branch
# consumes the token before the word, which must not be a negation,
# and the filter narrows the finding back to the word. A space in a
# pattern is itself a whitespace token, so `!@neg @word` reads a word,
# a space, and the minimizer. That branch needs a token before the
# word, so a sentence that opens with `simply` or `merely` counts
# through the other two, which need a verb or a glued comma after it
# (`Simply run make`, `Simply, the cache is a map`). Those two also
# fire after a negation when a verb follows (`is not simply copied`),
# a shape rare enough to leave.
#
# The other words have no opener branch, so a sentence that opens with
# one passes (`Just run make`). A branch for `just` would also fire
# after a negation (`you can't just use a container`), where it
# carries the contrast. `just` counts only before an imperative verb,
# since it also means `only` or `a moment ago` (`just one entry`, `the
# file was just created`). `is easy to` and `it's easy to` do not need a
# guard, since a negation splits them (`is not easy to`).
expr neg [not, never, isn't, aren't, wasn't, weren't, can't, cannot, couldn't, won't, doesn't, don't, didn't, shouldn't]
expr verbs [run, use, add, call, set, pass, type, install, copy, edit, change, create, delete, remove, update, point, drop, put, write, open, click]
expr word [(simply!(( )put)), merely, easily, trivially, straightforward, (easy to use), (just @verbs)]
expr main [<(!@neg @word), @word>, (simply[(( )<VERB, !put>), (,)]), (merely[(( )VERB), (,)]), (is easy to), (it's easy to)]
let message "Minimizer. Delete the word and let the reader judge the weight."
let description "Flags simply, merely, easily, and similar words that tell the reader a step is easy or a behavior is small."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "Flush writes the buffer to disk."
allows "Simply put, the cache is a map."
allows "The offset is not simply an index into the token list."
allows "It isn't merely a cache."
allows "WriteValue cannot easily tell whether the input aliases the buffer."
allows "The migration is not straightforward."
allows "You can't just use a container for this."
allows "The file was just created."
